      "message": "Objects whose count reached zero wait in the zero-count table until the next epoch, which comes after about 1 MiB of heap allocation. `collect_garbage` does nothing while Scheme runs, so a program can\u0027t force one. Meanwhile their slots still count.\n\nA loop that keeps at most one syntax object, `(datum-\u003esyntax context i)` in a loop, fails at a cap of baseline + 1000 after 1,000 iterations under deferred and precise counting. Without the cap it ends with one live slot. The test hides this by calling `collect_garbage()` between rounds.\n\nTwo things would help:\n- Charge the Rust side with `S::charge`, as `load_transient` does for a module, so syntax-heavy code brings epochs sooner.\n- At the cap, run an epoch (when not inside one) and check again before failing. Then the cap means \"live\", as ADR 0023 item 6 and the as-built note say.",
      "revId": "807b57e0afaf1b38149d52cf4674818b14087ed7",
